   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Hierarchical regression with a within-subjects factor",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>On Apr 2, 2014, at 1:18 AM, Andrew Ellis &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">a.w....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>\r<br>&gt; I recently asked this question on Cross-validated, but I think I&#39;ll have more luck here, as it&#39;s probably too software-related:\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "We&#39;re very friendly.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; I have data from a psychophysical experiment, in which I tested S subjects in J=2 conditions (within-subjects design). I am trying to model each subject&#39;s probability of giving a (binary) response y as a function of a stimulus parameter x; a standard approach is to use the cumulative normal density function as a psychometric function, which means that I&#39;m using a probit regression model.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Each observation is modelled as a mixture of Bernoulli distributions: either the subject does as he/she is supposed to or has an attentional lapse with a certain probability and then has to guess:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;  y[n] ~ bernoulli((1-lapse[subj[n]]) * Phi(alpha[subj[n], cond[n]] +\r<br>&gt;  beta[subj[n], cond[n]] * x[n]) +\r<br>&gt;  lapse[subj[n]] * guess[subj[n]]);\r<br>&gt;  }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Intercepts and slopes are allowed to vary by condition nested within subject. My aim is to estimate group-level intercept and slope parameters for each condition. \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The problem I&#39;m having is how to allow for the fact that alpha and beta are correlated, and, additionally, the parameters for each condition are correlated for each subject, i.e. if subject A has a high alpha in condition 1, then also in condition 2.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Can&#39;t you just build a hierarchical model of the various effects,\r<br>such as subject-level and conditional level?  The only thing to be\r<br>careful about is to not overparameterize with too many degrees of\r<br>freedom.\r<br>\r<br>The details obviously depend on the particular problem and what the\r<br>scales of alpha and beta are, but basically it&#39;s just a probit\r<br>regression.  You can also use logit --- it&#39;s a bit more efficient in Stan\r<br>(those cumulative distribution functions are expensive) and should\r<br>provide near identical results.\r<br>\r<br>This is easier to work with than trying to model the correlations directly.\r<br>Stan, at least, doesn&#39;t give you good ways to structure the covariance\r<br>matrix to model these effects directly in a correlation-based prior.\r<br>\r<br>There are lots of examples in Gelman and Hill&#39;s multilevel regression book.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
